+## Why every pod target is raised to the app's deployment target
+
+[[mobile/app.config.ts#withPodDeploymentTargetFloor]] adds a step to the Podfile's `post_install` hook. The step raises every pod target below the app's iOS deployment target to that target.
+
+`expo-build-properties` sets the Podfile's `platform :ios` line, and CocoaPods applies that minimum to each pod's library target. It does not apply it to resource bundle targets. A bundle target such as `Sentry-Sentry` or `RNSVG-RNSVGFilters` keeps the minimum its own podspec declares, which was 11.0 and 12.4.
+
+The iPhoneOS 27.2 SDK that came with the Xcode update of 2026-11-16 supports deployment targets from 15.0 only. A target below that range now fails the archive with `ARCHIVE FAILED` and exit status 65. Earlier SDKs gave only a warning for it. The step changes only targets below the app's value. It leaves a target with no explicit setting as it is, because that target inherits the project value.
+
+The step looks in `installer.generated_projects` and not only in `pods_project`. In that way it also reaches pods that CocoaPods puts in their own projects. The value comes from the same constant that is given to `expo-build-properties`, so the pods cannot fall behind the app. The plugin stops `prebuild` with an error if it does not find a `post_install` block, because without the step the archive fails much later.
+
 ## Deployment identifiers come from the environment
 
 The Apple team id, the Expo account that owns the project and the EAS project id are read from `APPLE_TEAM_ID`, `EXPO_OWNER` and `EAS_PROJECT_ID` rather than written into `app.config.ts`, `app.json` or `eas.json`.
